[38;2;124;124;124mThe degree of $H|_C$ on $C$ is the intersection number $H \cdot C$ on $S$. Since[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$C$ is a hyperplane section, $C \sim H$ on $S$, so $H \cdot C = H^2$. Since $S$ [0m[38;2;124;124;124mis canonically embedded, $H \sim K_S$, hence $H^2 = K_S^2$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore $\deg(H|_C) = K_S^2$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me also verify via adjunction: $K_C = (K_S + C)|_C = (K_S + H)|_C = 2H|_C$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(using $K_S = H$). So $\deg K_C = 2\deg(H|_C)$, i.e., $\deg(H|_C) = \frac{1}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m{2}\deg K_C$. And $\deg K_C = K_C \cdot C$... no, $\deg K_C = 2g(C) - 2$. Also [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mby adjunction on $S$: $2g(C) - 2 = (K_S + C)\cdot C = 2K_S \cdot C = 2K_S^2$. So[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$\deg(H|_C) = K_S \cdot C = K_S^2$. Consistent.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the answer is $\boxed{K_S^2}$.[0m

